.TH RANDOM_R 3
.SH NAME
random_r, srandom_r, initstate_r, setstate_r \- reentrant random number generator
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ", " \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.BI "int random_r(struct random_data *" buf ", int32_t *" result ");"
.br
.BI "int srandom_r(unsigned int " seed ", struct random_data *" buf ");"
.br
.BI "int initstate_r(unsigned int " seed ", char *" statebuf ,
.BI "size_t " statelen ", struct random_data *" buf ");"
.br
.BI "int setstate_r(char *" statebuf ", struct random_data *" buf ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are the reentrant equivalents of the
.BR random (3)
family.  All state lives in the caller-supplied
.I buf
and the caller-supplied
.I statebuf
array, so independent streams and threads do not collide.
.PP
.B initstate_r
binds
.I buf
to the
.IR statelen -byte
.I statebuf
and seeds it with
.IR seed .
The array size selects the generator quality, exactly as
.BR initstate (3)
documents: 8 bytes gives a simple linear-congruential generator, while 32, 64,
128 (the default) and 256 bytes select successively better additive-feedback
generators of degree 7, 15, 31 and 63.
.PP
.B srandom_r
re-seeds an already-initialized
.IR buf .
.B setstate_r
switches
.I buf
to a different, previously initialized
.IR statebuf .
.B random_r
stores the next value, uniformly in [0, 2^31), through
.IR result .
.SH RETURN VALUE
These functions return 0 on success.  On error they return \-1 with
.I errno
set to
.BR EINVAL .
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
A NULL argument was supplied, the
.I statebuf
passed to
.B initstate_r
was smaller than 8 bytes, or
.I buf
was not properly initialized.
.SH EXAMPLE
.RS 4
.EX
struct random_data rd;
char state[128];
int32_t v;
memset(&rd, 0, sizeof rd);
initstate_r(1, state, sizeof state, &rd);
random_r(&rd, &v);
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR random (3),
.BR drand48_r (3),
.BR rand (3)
.SH STANDARDS
GNU extension (the reentrant forms of
.BR random (3)).
